# Expected deviation from the exact result, in ulp, for the whole library.
#
# One file for all architectures, on purpose.  uClibc-ng's math functions are
# the same C code everywhere; what differs between targets is whether the
# compiler contracts a multiply and an add into one instruction and how wide the
# registers are.  Those differences are what a test suite should show, so they
# do not belong in a table.  A target that exceeds an entry here is a finding:
# look at it before raising the number, and write down what came out.
#
# What was here before were glibc's twenty-one per-architecture files, measured
# on glibc's implementations.  They were wrong for us in both directions -- too
# tight where our fdlibm is weaker (lgamma allowed 1 while ours reaches 8 over a
# dense sweep) and too loose elsewhere -- and libm-test-ulps-generic, the
# fallback for the eight architectures that had no file of their own, contained
# no data at all, which demanded a perfect result from hppa, nios2, alpha,
# sparc64, bfin, c6x, cris and avr32.
#
# Measured with UCLIBC_LIBM_SMALL, the default and the least accurate of the
# three implementations, so that the numbers hold for every setting.  The sharp
# per-setting check lives in tst-exp-ulp.c and its siblings, which know which
# implementation was built and allow it much less.
#
# Regenerate: build the suite with this file empty, run each of test-double,
# test-float, test-idouble and test-ifloat with -u (a static build runs under
# qemu-user), then merge with
#     perl ./gen-libm-test.pl -u ULPs -n -o "./"
# which writes NewUlps.  Note the trailing slash: -o "." writes ".NewUlps".

# cos
Test "cos (M_PI_6l * 2.0) == 0.5":
double: 1
idouble: 1
Test "cos (M_PI_6l * 4.0) == -0.5":
double: 2
float: 1
idouble: 2
ifloat: 1

